**Application decisions**

- Reuse the existing repository, App Router, TypeScript, and Tailwind. Use a restrained office interface with searchable tables, clear forms, confirmation dialogs, and prominent overdue/paperwork indicators.
- Use Next.js server functions for validation and record changes, with a user-authenticated Supabase client. Enforce permissions again in PostgreSQL and Storage.
- Use **Cloudflare Workers with Vinext**, Cloudflare’s currently recommended Next.js-compatible deployment path. Retain conventional Next.js development scripts. Compatibility with this repository must pass the first implementation gate; Vinext is currently beta. [Cloudflare guidance](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/framework-guides/web-apps/nextjs/)
- Use **Supabase Cron → Edge Function → Resend** for reminders, keeping background processing independent of the application host. [Scheduling documentation](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/functions/schedule-functions)
- Upload files directly to private Supabase Storage through narrowly authorized uploads, avoiding file buffering in Cloudflare.
- No public registration. Thadd provisions/disables accounts through Supabase initially. Staff can change their own email/password through Supabase Auth.
- Every data operation requires an authenticated, active staff record. Removing staff access takes effect through database policies even if an Auth session remains valid.

**Routes**

`/` shows work needing attention. `/clients`, `/calls`, `/projects`, `/tasks`, and `/documents` provide the five core views. `/projects/[id]` contains intake, tasks, documents, and deadlines. `/presets` manages reusable definitions; `/account` manages login details.

## 2. Database schema and access contracts

Keep the existing `clients.id` bigint key. Use UUIDs for new entity IDs, UTC timestamps for events, and PostgreSQL `date` values for office deadlines. Foreign keys preserve linked history; application deletion is archival.

| Tables | Principal fields and relationships |
|---|---|
| `staff` | ID, optional unique Auth user ID, display name, email, active flag, administrator flag. Historical staff can exist without login access. |
| `clients` | Existing fields plus company, cell phone, billing address, contact notes, referral source, optional unique office client number, archival fields. |
| `projects` | Client FK, office prefix, legacy project number, property address, details, service selections, status, notes, timeframe, first-contact/proposal/filing dates, import provenance, historical-completion flag. Legacy project numbers are **not unique keys**. |
| `project_staff` | Project/staff join table supporting multiple assignments. |
| `call_logs` | Caller/contact details, call time, summary, recording staff, optional client/project links, deletion timestamp and actor. |
| `call_updates` | Call FK, follow-up text, author, timestamp, deletion timestamp and actor. Original conversation and later outcomes remain separate. |
| `preset_tasks` | Name, description, work category, completion/document requirements, intake-field definitions, revision, archival fields. |
| `preset_groups`, `preset_group_items` | Named groups, group intake fields, ordered references to reusable tasks, revision. |
| `project_preset_applications` | Project FK, source preset/revision, immutable definition snapshot, intake answers, application timestamp and actor. |
| `tasks`, `task_staff` | Project FK, description, fieldwork/paperwork/other category, status, due date, multiple assignees, required flag, source snapshot reference, completion metadata. |
| `documents` | Project FK, title, category, optional subtype, archival fields. |
| `document_versions` | Document FK, immutable object path, original filename, media type, byte size, uploader, upload state/time, filing/hearing/issuance/closure dates. |
| `document_requirements` | Project FK, optional task/deadline FK, required document description/category, verified document-version FK, verifying staff and timestamp. |
| `deadlines` | Project FK, permit/final-sign-off type, label, due date, three notice offsets, revision, satisfied timestamp and actor. |
| `deadline_recipients` | Deadline/staff join table; only active staff with email addresses are selectable. |
| `reminder_deliveries` | Deadline/revision, stage, occurrence date, effective recipient, immutable email payload, state, attempt count, lease/retry timestamps, provider ID. Unique occurrence key prevents duplicate scheduling. |
| `reminder_attempts` | Delivery FK, attempt time, result/error, provider response identifiers. |
| `import_runs`, `import_rows` | Workbook checksum, sheet/row coordinates, original values, mapped records, validation flags, counts. |
| `audit_events` | Actor, action, entity, timestamp, and relevant change metadata. Staff cannot alter audit history. |

**Database-enforced interfaces**

- `apply_preset(...)` atomically snapshots definitions, validates supplied intake answers, and creates independent project tasks/requirements.
- `verify_document_requirement(...)` verifies that the uploaded version belongs to the same project and records staff attestation.
- `complete_project(...)` rejects completion while required intake answers, tasks, document verification, or deadline paperwork remain outstanding.
- Reminder claim/result functions provide transactional delivery leasing and status updates.
- Database constraints/triggers enforce these rules against direct database API calls as well as the interface.

All active staff may manage operational records and presets. Staff cannot activate accounts, grant themselves administrator access, edit audit records, or invoke privileged reminder operations.

## 3. Required behavior and integration details

**Clients, calls, and project completion**

- Archive/restore clients without removing their projects or call history. Archiving a client does not silently cancel outstanding project reminders.
- Call follow-ups are timestamped additions. Deletion requires confirmation and leaves a visible tombstone with deletion time and actor; authorized staff can inspect retained history.
- Keep fieldwork and paperwork as distinct tasks. Task defaults are To do, In progress, Blocked, Awaiting paperwork, and Completed.
- Signed paperwork means an uploaded document that staff explicitly verify; V1 does not collect signatures or infer them from file contents.
- Invalidating required evidence reopens the affected requirement and any verified completion dependent on it.

**Presets and intake**

- Every active staff member can create, edit, archive, and apply tasks/groups.
- Support text, multiline text, number, date, choice, and yes/no intake fields, with labels, stable IDs, required flags, ordering, and choice options.
- Applying a preset copies its tasks, requirements, and field definitions. Later template edits affect subsequent applications only.
- Allow incomplete intake drafts; identify missing required answers and block project completion.
- Search client/project details and saved intake labels/answers. No conditional form logic or general workflow builder in V1.

**Documents**

- Categories: Proposal, Transmittal, Filings, Permits, Compliance docs, Other.
- Accept PDF, DOCX, XLSX, JPG/JPEG, PNG, DWG, DXF, and ZIP, capped at **25,000,000 bytes per file**.
- Store each replacement as an immutable version. All active staff may archive/restore documents; no permanent-delete interface.
- Authorize upload initiation and finalization; verify stored size before marking a version available. Use generated storage paths rather than user-supplied paths.
- Authorize each download request and issue a short-lived signed URL. Download CAD/ZIP files as attachments.
- Verification refers to a particular version; replacing a document does not automatically verify its replacement.

**Spreadsheet import**

- Import 557 project records.
- Preserve each project row separately, including repeated project numbers and addresses. Group clients only by an identical trimmed source client label; flag possible identity collisions rather than using fuzzy matching.
- Retain all original values and sheet/row provenance. Preserve invalid dates as source text with review flags; leave the structured date empty.
- Preserve original status text alongside normalized display status. Do not infer completion from the sheet name.
- Mark imported completion claims as **historical, unverified**. New completion transitions require the current evidence checks.
- Preserve non-person assignment labels such as “Field” as source information; do not create login accounts from spreadsheet names.
- Do not invent expiration dates from filing dates or notes. Importing historical records must not trigger a flood of reminders.
- Make rerunning the same workbook idempotent through checksum/sheet/row identity. Archive the two starter test clients.
- Keep the source workbook and generated import payloads out of Git.

**Reminder processing**

- Default notices: **30, 14, and 3 days before**; then daily overdue reminders at **9 a.m. America/New_York**. Offsets are editable.
- Run the job every five minutes; calculate office dates correctly across daylight-saving changes.
- Stop each deadline’s reminders when its own signed paperwork is verified.
- After downtime, send the most urgent currently applicable notice instead of all missed notices.
- Deadline edits invalidate pending deliveries and create a revised future schedule. Preserve completed send history.
- Atomically claim deliveries, persist the exact request, and use a stable Resend idempotency key. Retry transient failures after 5, 15, and 60 minutes.
- Flag ambiguous outcomes for review rather than blindly resending after Resend’s **24-hour idempotency window**. [Resend idempotency](https://resend.com/docs/dashboard/emails/idempotency-keys)
- Handle quota failures as deferred work. Resend Free currently permits **100 emails/day and 3,000/month**, with recipients counted separately. Show queue failures and quota deferrals in the app. [Resend limits](https://resend.com/docs/knowledge-base/account-quotas-and-limits)
- Demonstration mode redirects delivery to **revisa@rpi.edu**, deduplicating redirected recipients for the same notice. Preview mode displays emails without sending or marking them sent.

## 4. Verification, setup placeholders, and completion criteria

**Required verification**

- Anonymous users, inactive staff, and forged staff-role updates cannot access records or storage.
- Password login/logout and self-service credential changes work; public signup is disabled.
- Import creates exactly 557 source-linked projects; rerunning creates no duplicates; malformed values remain recoverable.
- Archival preserves links; call deletion leaves timestamps/history.
- Applying then editing a preset leaves earlier project tasks and intake definitions unchanged.
- Fieldwork completion cannot complete a project with missing signed paperwork. Cross-project document evidence is rejected.
- Upload limits, version history, archival, and private downloads work.
- Concurrent reminder runs produce one delivery per occurrence; retries, deadline edits, verification, DST, quota failures, and uncertain provider outcomes are covered.
- Lint, type checking, meaningful automated tests, conventional Next.js build, Worker build, and browser walkthrough pass.

Cloudflare Free currently allows **10 ms CPU per request**. Test representative authentication and project screens early; move import and reminder work off the Worker as planned. A local compatibility pass does not prove live free-tier performance. [Cloudflare limits](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/)